Upstream's code, and why it was rewritten
-----------------------------------------

Upstream's repository carries the GPL-2.0 text, added in a commit titled
"添加 GPL 2.0 许可证" (add the GPL 2.0 licence), and says nothing about later
versions. Section 9 of GPL-2.0 allows any version only when the program "does
not specify a version number". Whether shipping the version 2 text under that
title specifies one is open to argument. Only the narrower reading cannot turn
out to be wrong, so upstream's code was treated as GPL-2.0-only.

Three files carried over upstream's lines: opencv.py, pagetree.py and
.gitignore. Each of them was GPL-2.0-only as a whole, and so was the program.
That closed two routes. OpenCV is licensed under Apache-2.0, which the FSF
considers compatible with GPLv3 but not with GPLv2, and libraries under
LGPL-3.0 cannot be combined with GPL-2.0-only code either. The fork's own work
was always offered under GPL-2.0-or-later to keep those routes open.

On 2026-10-05 the three files were rewritten, so that the whole tree is
GPL-2.0-or-later:

  - opencv.py was written anew from a behaviour specification, which was
    itself derived from the tests and the callers, without opening the previous
    implementation. Only after the new module passed the tests was it compared
    with the old one: to check that no structure had carried over beyond what
    the shared interface forces, and to keep one functional constant, the
    blank-frame cut-off, where it was.
  - pagetree.py: upstream's two tables, the list of template files and the
    page enumeration, were replaced. The file list is now collected from the
    rule tree, and the page values are generated from the member names. The
    rule tree, the action table and the result-page set were the fork's own
    work and stayed.
  - .gitignore was rebuilt from standard ignore patterns and the fork's own
    rules and comments. It ignores exactly the same paths as before.

This is not a clean-room reimplementation in the strict sense. The rewrite was
made by the same AI assistant that wrote the fork's earlier changes, and it may
have seen upstream's code before. The steps above are what was done to keep
the new code independent of it.

Upstream's code is still in the history before that date, under GPL-2.0-only,
together with the licence text it was distributed with (LICENSE). That includes
the Windows app layer parked there (main.py, option.py, hook/gbfr_hook.c and
icon.ico): restoring those files as they are, as docs/provenance/README.md
explains, would bring GPL-2.0-only code back into the tree.


Modified files
--------------

GPL-2.0 section 2(a) asks that modified files carry prominent notices saying
that they were changed, and when. No current file is a modified version of an
upstream file any more. The git history records every change and its date, and
docs/provenance/ maps the commits made before this repository existed back to
the archive.


About the AI-written code
-------------------------

The fork's changes and this repository's code were written by an AI coding
assistant (Anthropic's Claude Code) at the owner's direction, and were not
reviewed line by line by a human. Whether AI-generated output is protected by
copyright is unsettled; to the extent it is, the notices above apply. The
README says more, including what has been verified and how.
